WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.550
- [Interviewer] All right, Chelsey.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.000
Chelsey, where'd you grow up,
where are you from originally?

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.060
- Virginia.
- What part of Virginia?

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.210
- Pocahontas, Virginia.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.550
- [Interviewer] Pocahontas.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:11.563
Oh, I've been there.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:13.900
I just came back from there.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.563
- There's less than 500 people.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.673
- [Interviewer] That was incredible.

00:00:17.673 --> 00:00:18.770
Yeah, we just drove through there.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.883
I could do a whole
story just on that town.

00:00:20.883 --> 00:00:23.080
It's a ghost town now.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.940
- Pretty much.
- So what was the,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.960
it was coal, was your
industry at one time?

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.550
- Yes.
- 'Cause there's a downtown,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.220
there's a whole, it's
like an entire small town

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.370
with a grocery store, and all
the buildings and everything,

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.300
and it's deserted.
- Mm-hmm.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.784
- [Interviewer] There's not
a single business there.

00:00:39.784 --> 00:00:41.630
- The post office and the bank.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:45.700
- [Interviewer] So the coal
industry left and the town is--

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.723
- It's just residents now.
- Yeah, and nobody works.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.110
How long ago did you leave?

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.763
- I left,

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:57.973
2012.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.300
- [Interviewer] What was going on in 2012?

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.250
- My mom passed away.
- Oh, I'm sorry.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:06.793
- She had cancer.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.603
She passed away at 43.
- I'm sorry.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:21.663
- (sighs)
- It's hard.

00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:22.500
I just lost my mom, too.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.250
So you, tell me about your childhood.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.243
Did you have both your
parents when you were a kid?

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.750
- My dad was in and out a lot.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.363
My mom, she was always there.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.473
My mom was my best friend.

00:01:43.253 --> 00:01:45.180
- [Interviewer] And you had siblings?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.073
- I had three sisters and a brother.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.193
- [Interviewer] The childhood was good?

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.013
- I was raped from the age of eight to 16.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.703
By my dad's dad.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.183
- [Interviewer] Your grandfather.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:03.990
Did you ever tell anybody?

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.653
- Not until I was 16.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.163
I had had my first kid.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.933
He had cholic bad.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.447
And I was up for three days
with him and I thought,

00:02:19.447 --> 00:02:21.797
"if somebody'd done this
to him, I'd kill 'em."

00:02:25.279 --> 00:02:26.860
And I finally said something.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.903
- [Interviewer] So, who is
the father of your child?

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.820
- Not my grandfather.
- Not your grandfather.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:32.653
- No.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:36.653
- [Interviewer] And you,

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.710
did anything happen to your
grandfather after that?

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.083
- He did four years in prison.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:49.053
It's like, that's nowhere
near what he done to me.

00:02:51.612 --> 00:02:52.662
It just seems unfair.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:54.733
But...

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.133
- [Interviewer] So you,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.093
where'd your life go
after that all happened?

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.790
You had a child, how many
children do you have now?

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.190
- I have three.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:08.223
Three boys.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.033
When my mom died,

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.423
I was going through a
custody battle with my kids.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:19.743
And,

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.743
my youngest two's dad was getting high.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.950
I was always around it,
but I wouldn't do it.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.783
But the day my mom died, I did.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.183
I quit going to court for my kids.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.503
My whole life was to make my mom happy.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.480
When she died, I didn't care anymore.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.520
So I left home, I went to Denver

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.950
for a year, and then I went back home

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.003
for like, two or three.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.153
Seen my sister.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.453
And then this last time I
left, I went back to Denver.

00:04:14.302 --> 00:04:16.510
And I've been in California

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.090
going on three years now.
- So you went to Colorado

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:21.333
and you left your children behind?

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.233
- My sister,

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:30.327
my sister's mother-in-law
got custody of 'em.

00:04:33.827 --> 00:04:36.130
- [Interviewer] And drugs were
in your life at that point

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:37.910
for the whole period?

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.310
- Yeah.
- What's your drug?

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.313
- Then, it was crack.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.500
And then, I switched

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:47.773
to crystal.

00:04:50.002 --> 00:04:53.827
But I haven't, the crack
was my, my drug of choice.

00:04:55.507 --> 00:04:56.757
Like, it took everything.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.093
- [Interviewer] You stay
in touch with your kids?

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.470
- As much as my sister will let me.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:19.430
- [Interviewer] And then, how
were you supporting yourself

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.103
when you were in Colorado?

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.063
- I had a job for a little bit at a hotel.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.163
And prostitution.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.350
I haven't, my kids' dad, he would

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:41.790
do his little thing to make money,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.800
but when me and him split up, I just

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:48.650
had a couple pimps I worked for.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.480
And then when I got here, I met

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.503
this guy named Trey.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.340
He actually kept me from killing myself.

00:06:07.368 --> 00:06:09.077
- [Interviewer] Are you depressed a lot?

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:11.410
(sniffling)

00:06:20.486 --> 00:06:22.110
(sobbing)

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:22.943
- Thank you.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:30.320
- [Interviewer] What's
the hardest part for you,

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.870
being separated from your kids?

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.770
- That, and my mom.
- And your mom, yeah.

00:06:43.693 --> 00:06:45.860
(sighing)

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:50.203
- I'm the oldest out
of all of my siblings.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.700
And all my other siblings took it hard,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.083
but not as hard as I did.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.913
My youngest sister
started going to church.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.983
And I completely went in
the opposite direction.

00:07:10.773 --> 00:07:13.106
(sniffling)

00:07:14.197 --> 00:07:15.030
- [Interviewer] The sexual abuse you had

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.440
when you were young, was that something

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:17.953
your sisters went through, too?

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.400
- One of my sisters, the one that's

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.860
two years younger than me.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.760
But she told the same day it happened.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:30.683
And,

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.310
he,

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.163
he got put on probation or,

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.843
it was like, nothing.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:46.393
And,

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.853
the way my family treated her,

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:54.940
I was like, I can't say anything.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.720
- [Interviewer] How did they treat her?

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:00.553
- My dad,

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.767
he was like, "you're a liar.

00:08:04.767 --> 00:08:06.477
"You're just doing it for money."

00:08:07.881 --> 00:08:09.031
We didn't get any money

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.103
to tell what really happened.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.690
They said some really
hurtful things about her.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:20.697
- [Interviewer] So that made it difficult

00:08:20.697 --> 00:08:22.017
for you to come out.

00:08:25.607 --> 00:08:26.530
- And when I finally did,

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:28.663
that's the first person I told, was her.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.370
'Cause it was, I was like, "I'm sorry

00:08:35.437 --> 00:08:37.107
"that I didn't stick up for you."

00:08:39.639 --> 00:08:41.743
(sniffling) And now, we don't even talk.

00:08:49.465 --> 00:08:51.798
(sniffling)

00:08:58.184 --> 00:08:59.017
- [Interviewer] The drug use helps you

00:08:59.017 --> 00:09:00.413
deal with it all, right?

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.570
- Not necessarily deal with it, but

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.240
not think about it as much.
- Not deal with it, yeah.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.600
- 'Cause whenever you
come down, it's like,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.410
it's 10 times more than
what it would've been

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.123
if you would've just dealt with it.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.393
- [Interviewer] So then,
you came to LA at one point.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.550
How long you been here?

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:28.993
- Going on three years.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.183
- [Interviewer] And you've
been staying on Skid Row?

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:35.603
